{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"account-default","id":"msg_011Cgyp8mgZDb8Rc5zNgmLX2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QA5jswWUtSJBiutuNQm5nv","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"verdict":"needs-human","reasons":["The auto-approve rule doesn't apply. That rule only covers plans that add or tag resources and don't touch buyer records. This plan touches buyer records, and one of its changes deletes a resource.","aws_s3_bucket.buyer_records is planned as delete+create, not an in-place change. Renaming the bucket from 'tallowmark-prod-buyer-records' to 'tallowmark-prod-buyer-records-v2' forces Terraform to destroy the prod buyer records bucket and create a new one. The data is not migrated. If force_destroy is set, the existing buyer records are lost permanently. If not, the apply fails partway through, while the bucket is non-empty.","This loss can't be undone. Versioning on the old bucket was 'Suspended', so there may be no prior object versions to recover from. The versioning change to 'Enabled' only affects the new bucket, which starts empty.","No block rule applies. There are no IAM policies with wildcard actions or resources, and no database changes. The plan also isn't telemetry or scan-reading lifecycle housekeeping.","A person should confirm the rename is intended. If it is, they should require a migration plan before anyone applies: either copy the data to the new bucket and verify it, or keep the existing bucket and import it into Terraform under its current name instead of recreating it. Adding lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true } to this bucket is also recommended."]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":822,"cache_read_input_tokens":3546,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":822},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"789a9698-d66d-44cf-8338-d2e9ebcd43f7","uuid":"93600d31-37aa-4b67-9529-25c1177a7ac1","timestamp":"2026-11-12T22:21:24.236Z","request_id":"req_011Cgyp8mV9dPexw1PkBpMU4","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QA5jswWUtSJBiutuNQm5nv":{"verdict":"needs-human","reasons":["The auto-approve rule doesn't apply. That rule only covers plans that add or tag resources and don't touch buyer records. This plan touches buyer records, and one of its changes deletes a resource.","aws_s3_bucket.buyer_records is planned as delete+create, not an in-place change. Renaming the bucket from 'tallowmark-prod-buyer-records' to 'tallowmark-prod-buyer-records-v2' forces Terraform to destroy the prod buyer records bucket and create a new one. The data is not migrated. If force_destroy is set, the existing buyer records are lost permanently. If not, the apply fails partway through, while the bucket is non-empty.","This loss can't be undone. Versioning on the old bucket was 'Suspended', so there may be no prior object versions to recover from. The versioning change to 'Enabled' only affects the new bucket, which starts empty.","No block rule applies. There are no IAM policies with wildcard actions or resources, and no database changes. The plan also isn't telemetry or scan-reading lifecycle housekeeping.","A person should confirm the rename is intended. If it is, they should require a migration plan before anyone applies: either copy the data to the new bucket and verify it, or keep the existing bucket and import it into Terraform under its current name instead of recreating it. Adding lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true } to this bucket is also recommended."]}}}
